---
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
import Nav from '../components/Nav.astro';
import Footer from '../components/Footer.astro';
---
<Base
  title="Privacy Policy · Datawake"
  description="Datawake's privacy policy. Short version: we don't collect your data. Everything the extension records stays on your device."
>

<Nav />

<div class="page-hero">
  <div class="container">
    <h1>The short version: we don't collect your data.</h1>
    <p>Datawake works entirely inside your browser. What it sees and records stays on your device, and we run no server that receives any of it. This policy explains exactly what the extension looks at and why.</p>
    <p style="font-size:13px; color:var(--text-muted); margin-top:16px;">Last updated: 9 October 2026</p>
  </div>
</div>

<div class="container policy-content">

  <h2>What Datawake does</h2>
  <p>Datawake is a browser extension that shows you which companies track you on the sites you visit, says no to cookie banners for you, and checks whether sites respect that choice. It runs entirely in your browser. Nothing it records is sent to us.</p>

  <h2>What we collect</h2>
  <p><strong>Nothing.</strong> We do not operate any server that receives data from your copy of Datawake. We cannot see the sites you visit, the trackers you meet, your settings or anything else in the extension. The extension contains no analytics, crash reporting or telemetry.</p>

  <h2>What the extension looks at, and why</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Requests to other companies' servers.</strong> To see which third parties load on a page and name the company behind them. Only the domain is used, not the content.</li>
    <li><strong>Cookies.</strong> To tell whether a site set tracking cookies after you clicked Reject. Datawake compares cookie names and domains. It never stores cookie values.</li>
    <li><strong>Cookie banners.</strong> To click "Reject" for you, if you leave that setting on, and to notice when a site only lets you refuse if you pay.</li>
    <li><strong>How scripts use certain browser features.</strong> To detect fingerprinting, Datawake notes when a script reads things like canvas drawings or installed fonts. It records which script did it and which technique, not what the script learned.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>Datawake also sends the Global Privacy Control signal to websites, a standard way of saying you do not want your data sold or shared. You can turn this off in Settings.</p>

  <h2>What stays on your device</h2>
  <p>The following is stored in your browser's own storage and never leaves it:</p>
  <ul>
    <li>Tracker domains and the companies behind them, with the site and time they were seen</li>
    <li>What Datawake did for you: banners rejected, pay walls and fingerprinting noticed</li>
    <li>Violations: the names and domains of cookies set after you said no, and any fingerprinting</li>
    <li>Sites you paused, and sites remembered because rejecting there led to a pay wall</li>
    <li>If you use them: your name, email, postal address and country for GDPR letters and complaints, the companies you add as accounts, and your requests</li>
    <li>Your settings</li>
  </ul>
  <p>Tracking history and the record of what Datawake did are deleted automatically after 90 days. Violations are kept until you clear them, so you keep the evidence. You can clear everything at any time from Settings in the dashboard, and removing the extension deletes it all.</p>

  <h2>Letters and complaints</h2>
  <p>Datawake can write GDPR request letters to companies and complaints to a data protection authority. It only writes the text. Nothing is sent unless you send it yourself, by copying the text or opening it in your own email.</p>

  <h2>The data breach check</h2>
  <p>The optional breach check, on this website and in the extension, looks up the email address you type in the breach database run by XposedOrNot. Your browser sends it straight to them over an encrypted connection; it never passes through Datawake and we never store it. Their own privacy policy covers how they handle the lookup. This is the only time the extension contacts a server other than the websites you visit.</p>

  <h2>Datawake Pro</h2>
  <p>Datawake Pro is not available yet. Before it launches we will update this policy to explain exactly what is needed to run a subscription. The free extension will keep working without an account.</p>

  <h2>Open source</h2>
  <p>The extension's code is open source on <a href="https://github.com/DatawakeApp/datawake">GitHub</a>, so you can check everything in this policy for yourself.</p>

  <h2>This website</h2>
  <p>datawake.app is hosted on GitHub Pages and uses no analytics or tracking. GitHub may log technical information such as IP addresses for security; see GitHub's privacy statement. These logs are not linked to the extension in any way.</p>

  <h2>Your rights</h2>
  <p>Because we hold no personal data from the extension, there is nothing for us to access, correct or delete. Everything is on your device and under your control. If you write to us, we only use your message to reply, and you can ask us to delete it.</p>

  <h2>Changes to this policy</h2>
  <p>If we ever change how Datawake handles data, we will update this page before the change ships and note it in the extension's release notes. The current version is always at datawake.app/privacy.</p>

  <h2>Contact</h2>
  <p>Questions about privacy? Email <a href="mailto:privacy@datawake.app">privacy@datawake.app</a>.</p>

</div>

<Footer />

</Base>
